 little bit to the campus  experience did either of you have a  significant other or has a significant  other and can you talk a little bit  about the experience of coming to the  GSB with a partner sure yeah so my  experiences of maybe might be a little  bit different I my boyfriend is also a  student and Nick in my class I'm also an  MBA - currently and so it's you know I  think the first time that we were in  school together I think I would  definitely say it's it's tough because  as probably everyone's heard it's it can  get really busy so it's important to set  side time with one another but I think  for us you know we really viewed and I  think this is true for even couples  where one person is a student one person  is not it's really a mutual growing  experience and there's so many  opportunities for your partner to be  involved or not involved depending on  what kind of path you decide to carve  and it can be a huge learning experience  for both of you whether you're both  students or not and so I don't have an S  over while being here but I will add  there are a lot of other resources that  are available there are there's actually  clubs for eso's and you get integrated  within the campus so you're also  actually gets a card to go to the gym  and the sporting events that are here  and then even some of the most popular  classes within the GSB you're also can  take within their own sections so for  example touchy feely is a really well  known course here and they have special  sections for us so so I think there's a  lot of effort to culturally integrate  them into the community so it's kind of  one large class we also have a question  about financially Aidan is it offered to  international students so I can actually  take that one because they don't think  either of you or international students  but I we do offer need-based financial  aid for every single person who's  admitted to our program and we don't  differentiate between international  students or American students in terms  of access to financial aid but maybe  each of you could speak a little bit  about the cost benefit thoughts that  went through your mind when you were  pursuing an MBA and ways that you  financed it or thought about investing  in your education here at Stanford so I  came from a background of having done  four years of living and working in  sub-saharan Africa so I was a recipient  of some of the need-based financial aid  which very much made that a positive NPV  calculation for me it's amazing about  how much recruiters start coming to you  as a GSB student all the way through  on-campus recruiting to just random kind  of cold emails that happen during your  second year so the the bump in in salary  can be pretty significant and more  importantly maybe the salary but the  actual opportunities that you can do  well in some of these organizations  makes it MPD product positive I mean for  me within one or two  yeah I think for me I didn't receive  substantial fellowship actually very low  and have been relying primarily on loans  and so I think that cost benefit for me  it's actually still you know the benefit  is harder to measure at least in the  near term but for me the GSB has opened  up doors as Nick mentioned that I just  would never been able to walk through  before coming or even knowing that I  wanted to and I think before coming in  there was you know honestly a question  my mind of this is expensive you know  consulting is also general you can go to  something else and I think having this  hindsight bias now I'm really glad that  I did make that trade-off and I just  want to say too if you're not a  fellowship recipient the school and also  there are private resources are that are  both are really good and helping you  bridge that gap and it's what I rely on  for for my education great so aside from  great whether someone is asking us what  are some of the benefits of our location  where we are either geopolitically or in  terms of the valley  have you taken advantage of local  resources that are around campus from a  lifestyle perspective I love hiking and  if you haven't had a chance to come to  the bay and figure out like what  redwoods are they're these massive trees  some of the most beautiful hiking I  think in the world in terms of day hikes  are right around here San Francisco is  an amazing city just culturally it's  great Silicon Valley so I think the  benefit of the school is we get a lot of  entrepreneurs and venture capitalists  that come in to speak and teach classes  here that you probably wouldn't get  outside of the valley just cuz the  resources that are here yeah I would say  make pretty much covered it and just add  that for a lot of industries like  venture capital which I'm interested in  also tech but many more a health care  consumer there are so many interests  interesting exciting companies within an  hour drive of us and so I think getting  to visit them whether that's through  interviews or just learning has been  really  and you're kind of in the middle of it  so I would say something less tangible  is just the energy that you get from  that and you know I grew up in Chicago  went to school in Boston lived in New  York I do think it's pretty unique to  this area skiing and surfing is also  really accessible if you're in the  morning surf in the afternoon so along  the same lines having access to all of  these companies do you have any concrete  examples you really wanted to explore  impact investing and we have a question  about access to social entrepreneurship  and having been previously involved in  kind of social impact related work do  you have any thoughts on resources for  that in particular either within the  region or at in the Stanford MBA program  each one of you could touch briefly yeah  so I think you know I briefly mentioned  this before but I think the impact in  bet both impact investing and then also  just impact labs program is a program  offered by the GSB where you're placed  either with a nonprofit or an impact  investment fund and you get to intern  for them for school credit I would also  say more generally this that Social  Innovation is very very well resourced  at the GSB and at Stanford broadly so  there's a social innovation Club a  student Club but they're also you know  resources at the center that you can  take advantage of to test different  hypotheses that you want to so just add  on that the Center for Social Innovation  actually has GSB staff on that they have  trips of GST global study trips that  will focus specifically on Social  Innovation I was on one in Brazil and  then there's another program called seed  which has an acronym that I don't know  what it stands for but essentially is  money that's set aside to invest with  social entrepreneurs and that that  materializes a few ways from a student  experience so one is a lot of times  those social entrepreneurs from around  the world will be here on campus and you  have access to them and then secondly to  that there's a chance to to take funding  to work in social entrepreneurship as  part of your internship experience so if  an organization kind of can qualify  through stand  to accept funding it allows you to get  closer to market rate as a student to  work in social entrepreneurship over the  summer and then also they have a loan  forgiveness program to certain students  should you launch a career with a  non-profit and social entrepreneur  afterwards so I think there are lots of  lots of access and lots of resources and  I argue probably one of the core  competencies in the school there's  really concrete examples so tom has a  question for you guys about aspects of  your experience where you may have been  challenged beyond what you expected at  the GSB and what is the most important  piece of advice that you would give your  first year self looking back now so yeah  so I think the first thing is is be open  and ready to change so one of the the  great parts of the curriculum here have  to do a lot with interpersonal skills  and leadership development there are so  many mental models or there were so many  mental models that I held coming into  the GSB about what leadership is how  people react in different situations and  being in such a diverse environment and  having a very open culture of feedback  and the way the curriculum is set up you  really get to channel challenge those  mental models and I think some of the  most challenging parts were in a team  situation or in some of these courses  where you kind of really get down to  being having to be pretty vulnerable to  get to a point to get changed and become  a much more effective leader and so  being open to that process is really  really important and sometimes can take  a few weeks to get comfortable in yeah I  would think um sort of along this the  same theme before the GSB I wouldn't  consider myself a leader I was very good  at getting the job that was asking me  done and always at a junior level as an  analyst or associate and at the GSB it's  the first time I've ever in my life felt  like I have the confidence to be a  leader and I think that's actually been  associated with things that have been  challenging at the GSB so I'm one of the  co presidents of women and management  this year and helped lead a board of 10  people we plan retreats for the full  class and just thinking about thing  like how do you create a bored culture  how do you motivate people who are  volunteers and your friends but need to  get a lot of work done um you know among  our already busy schedule and I think  those are real things that managers and  leaders have to face and I've never  gotten the opportunity to do so and have  just learned so much from that  experience I think my advice to my first  year cell for any first years would be  be open like Nick said but also just  take the chance and have confidence in  yourself and I think having confidence  sometimes just comes from doing it  anyway even if you think you can't do it  and asking yourself why not you and I  think the GSB has made me ask myself  that over and over again and I've just  taken on way more than I would have  thought I could have that's not only  great advice or first year but probably  great advice for life so you touched a  little bit about the challenges of being  a club leader balancing that with a lot  of other obligations at school so we've  got a question about a typical week how  are you balancing cut and dry classwork  leadership opportunities through clubs  other you know initiatives that you  might be involved with how does that  break down and how do you end up  prioritizing your time sure so I guess  I'll preface of this with I was given  the advice and I haven't followed as  closely they should have but it's  definitely good advice to ruthlessly  prioritize and do that by coming in and  obviously being adaptive I'm inflexible  but coming in with a clear list of what  your prior priorities are and whether  that's academics or social job-related  being pretty specific about them just  coming in because I think a big struggle  at least for me at the GSB is not  letting the urgent get in the way of the  important so I would say the topic of  time management has been has been tough  for me because there are so many  interesting things to do here  in terms of a typical week and this kind  of ties back to my priorities which I  think number one were around meeting  people that's one of the big reasons I  came here just to get to experience the  people here  and then I would say for me and this I  might be a minority here but academics  are really important to me and I I love  I loved the classes that I've been in  and the professors that I've had so I  would say spent a fair amount of time on  academics and then you know I mentioned  women in management and a few other  extracurriculars and I'm in I would say  I pretty much spread my time among those  three and right now for me at least  extracurriculars are taking a bigger  role in my life I think for  prioritization I try to go back to my  list that I created and you know update  every now and then to say I said that  this is more important to me let me not  cancel on a meeting with a friend  because of this particular thing which  you know I know is less important and  yeah I'm trying to think about also how  that changed my first year - my second  year so first year I think academics  probably require a little bit more work  outside of the classroom then they do  your second year in your second year you  have all electives so also because  you're you've chosen every single class  you want to be in working for them  doesn't really feel like work it's it's  you know intellectually stimulating on  all levels and so I probably I don't  know could give an hour breakdown but  you know there is a decent amount of  time for a class prep meeting other  classmates and building relationships  with classmates to take a significant  amount of time but I mean that's  something I love doing I also need a lot  of personal time for myself so I block  out at least an hour a day just a that's  on my calendar that no one else can can  schedule over and so I there's kind of  there's a great way to balance academics  recruiting recruiting takes up a lot of  time depending which cycle you go  through and then you have just  prioritize at what quarter which of  those are most important for you to go  through have you thought about pursuing  a joint or dual degree I have not I  thought about doing the Masters of  Education okay  and I decided against it only just  because I mean and the reason I wanted  to do it was so that I could get an  extra quarter so I could get two  internships  and that really just kind of came back  to prioritizing because I was kind of  thinking did I want to do health or  education and just did kind of a  cost-benefit analysis and decided  against it yeah but we have obviously  lots of classmates that are doing JD MBA  s that we're doing MD MBAs and they seem  to really enjoy it  yeah and it's an important personal  calculus to make just like everything  else at the GSB it's kind of a  choose-your-own-adventure and you always  need to think through the trade-offs if  what do I gain from this and what are  the advantages of doing it or not doing  it did you dip your toes into the water  of maybe taking classes at the ad school  or have either of you taken classes what  we call across the street any of the  other grad schools at Stanford I am I  have not yet I'm hoping next quarter to  take what's called bio design which I  think it is actually a med school class  but it's cross-functional between  engineering medicine and business to  launch a medical device product yeah I  would say I'm in the same camp I've just  found there to be so many interesting  GSB classes that being said though  something that I came in really wanting  to get a better grasp of is computer  science and obviously Stanford is one of  the best computer science programs in  the world and so for next quarter I'm  signed up for a computer science class  and I think something that I would also  like to try is just more with the design  school which I think has that very  unique type of thinking then we can all  benefit from great I'm also taking rock  climbing next quarter I forgot to throw  that in there so there are lots of PE  classes that are everything from hip-hop  to rock climbing to horseback riding to  golf golf as a very very popular sunny  all the time here so I've gotten a few  questions for admission so I'm just  gonna address those really quickly one  was for students who apply from college  directly so if you're currently a senior  in college you're more than welcome to  apply to you go through the exact same  application process and you actually  we're looking for the same three  criteria that we talked about earlier in  the presentation so we're not looking  for anything different if you're  thinking about letters of recommendation  you might want to think about your  summer internships  faculty members tend not to be the best  recommenders in our view we're looking  for people who really can highlight your  professional competencies as well as  your leadership so if a professor was an  advisor to you for a club for example or  for your research that would be really  helpful but if it was just a  student-teacher relationship typically  that's where I see college seniors  benefiting more from using your  internship supervisors or you know other  people that have you've kind of been  held accountable to once you get in we  typically ask you to defer your  enrollment so you actually go and get  some work experience for a couple years  and then matriculate into the GSB after  those two or two years in terms of  making yourself competitive as an  undergrad the same advice actually goes  to everybody which is and you know we  are looking for people that demonstrate  a lot of intellectual curiosity so take  tough classes challenge yourself perform  well and then get involved in activities  in your community and demonstrate your  leadership potential those are things  we're looking for across all of our  candidates  similarly for joint and dual degree  programs we're not looking for any  separate admissions criteria we're  looking for the same three things for  our program however you will also have  to meet the admissions requirements for  those other programs so whatever the  requirements are say for law school or  medical school those are not things that  we would be looking for in our  candidates but they are things that you  would obviously have to meet for those  other programs we also had a question  about placing weight on every component  of the application itself for example do  we weigh the GPA versus test versus  recommendation letters differently and I  wish I could tell you that there was  some secret formula for how to read an  application and create a calculus for it  it would make my job way easier but  actually it's an art and a science so we  don't have any sort of equation that we  use so by definition there is no weight  placed on any one component we talked a  little bit earlier about how we have  holistic application process and what  that means fundamentally is that we look  at every single piece of your  application so every single thing counts  and everything is a part of our review  process we try to weave all three  criteria of intellect leadership and  personal qualities into our evaluation  of you as a candidate and we really pour  a lot of time and energy and  appreciation for how much you invest in  your application by taking a holistic  approach to it so there's no one piece  of your application that kind of makes  or breaks it even if you're invited to  interview it's not like the interview  tips you in or out that goes back in  your application which is really a 'td  as a whole so every single piece matters  and not one piece matters more than the  other so we're coming up on the last  five minutes and we had a student ask a  question that I think would be or not a  student participant asked a question  that could be a good capstone for us and  that is kind of have you had one  monumental experience so far at the GSB  and I can phrase that another way which  would be can you talk about your  favorite memory so far and it can be  anything it doesn't have to be academic  or if it is that's great but anything  about your experience that you would say  stands out as your favorite part so far  question actually I have one I have mine  I mentioned that I am co-leading women  in management this year and it's been a  really challenging experience part of  the reason that it was so challenging  was planning our annual fall retreat 160  women come together for a weekend and  really it's ours to plan we have last  year's curriculum but we decided to go  rogue this year and do it from scratch  and it was just it was incredibly  stressful the you know months leading up  to it and I would say it was the most  fulfilling moment for me at GSB because  of what happened that weekend and and  also you know just the I would  the discussions but really the sense of  community that came out of that I think  when you get to the GSB especially your  first year it's just kind of crazy and  in good ways but also not-so-great ways  and to the opportunity to provide a  retreat where for particularly first  year MBAs could hit the reset button and  say hey let's breathe let's pause and  let's remember why we came here and also  talk about what's holding us back from  being our fullest selves and really be  authentic and honest I got to facilitate  those sessions for the first time and  I'm just hearing those discussions come  out of that made all of the effort  really worth it and and I think just the  community element to inter class which I  wanted more of last year was really  special for me I think there had been a  lot of profounder impactful moments that  have happened within a lot of the  interpersonal dynamic classes and  leadership classes that I've had but  those are going to take a while to set  up so I say probably one of the more  like pinch myself moments was I was in a  class with dr. Condoleezza Rice who was  the former US Secretary of State and we  had a case simulation that she was  running about what to do from a business  standpoint you know in Eastern Europe  assuming this fictitious country invaded  this and you had resources in that  country and ended up getting into like a  very very small suit or debate about  like the tripwire theory of NATO which  was like crazy that you know this is one  of the architects for some of this and  an actual policymaker and still very  prominent on the world stage in that  side and I think that speaks a little  bit of the resources that you cut or the  access you can get in this type of  environment not happen like the third  week of class in my first year wow  that's that's really exciting well I  want to thank both of you so much for  taking the time I know we've talked  about priorities and how busy you guys  all are on campus so I just want to  thank you so much for sharing your  experience and really bringing the  Stanford MBA experience alive to  everybody who's been joining us and to  all of you who've been online with us I  really appreciate you spending your time  with us as well to learn more about the  Stanford MBA program
 

